<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Edison</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1847">1847</a>–<a href="/years/1931">1931</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Thomas Alva Edison, The Wizard of Menlo Park</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas Edison was an American inventor and businessman known for his work on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incandescent-light-bulb">incandescent light bulb</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phonograph">phonograph</a>, electric power distribution and early motion pictures. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, he worked as a railroad telegrapher before turning to invention, and developed Menlo Park, now considered the first industrial research laboratory. From 1878 he worked on an electrical lighting system to replace the gas lamp; competition with Westinghouse's AC system and the merger of Edison General Electric with Thomson-Houston led to the formation of General Electric. He registered 1,093 patents in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-edison.jpg" alt="Thomas Edison" width="330" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis Bachrach, Bachrach Studios, restored by Michel Vuijlsteke · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thomas_Edison2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Thomas Edison</b> lit his Menlo Park laboratory and nearby streets with incandescent lamps for crowds who came by special train.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-31">31 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Thomas Edison</b> spoke the nursery rhyme into his tinfoil phonograph and heard his voice played back.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-6">6 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Companies holding the telephone patents of <b>Thomas Edison</b> and Alexander Graham Bell combined to form the Oriental Telephone Company.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-25">25 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Thomas Edison</b> announced his invention of the phonograph, a machine that recorded sound on tinfoil and played it back.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-21">21 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Thomas Edison</b> filed a patent caveat describing an 'optical phonograph' to record and reproduce moving images.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-17">17 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At Menlo Park, <b>Thomas Edison</b>'s team lit a bulb with a carbonised cotton-thread filament that glowed for about 13.5 hours.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-21">21 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
